PM DOS API translator allows the protected-mode program to call DOS
services directly, via the familiar int 21h and similar techniques.
While it may be a part of the DOS extender, the common practice is to
put it directly into a DPMI server instead, to save the DOS extenders
a great deal of work. Unfortunately, many DPMI servers do not contain
the DOS API translator, and as a result, some DOS extenders fail to run.

This is a port of dosemu's DOS API translator, that can be installed as
a TSR to provide the API translation services to protected-mode programs.

Here it is linked into run286.exe, and "run286 /tsr" installs it. It is
then also what runs the programs bound to Phar Lap's 286|DOS-Extender,
with no restubbing: the extender enters DPMI as a 16-bit client, the
resident service provider call for it (rsp_call() in msdoshlp.c) marks
it, and on the extender's first interrupt call after that run286's loader
takes over, in the same client, on the same file. The client stays 16-bit:
pmdapi and run286 are 32-bit code, and with THUNK_16_32 the host gives
them 32-bit frames and entries by the bitness of their code segment,
THUNK_16_32x does the same in dosemu's own translator. Needs dosemu2's
DPMI 1.0 extensions (RSP with flags, THUNK_16_32/x, the LDT monitor).
